class Solution {
    public int findMin(int[] nums) {
        int start = 0;                  // Left boundary of search space
        int end = nums.length - 1;      // Right boundary

        // Continue until the search space reduces to one element
        while (start < end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;  // Prevents overflow

            // Case 1: Middle element is greater than the last element
            // → Minimum must be in the right half (unsorted part)
            if (nums[mid] > nums[end]) {
                start = mid + 1;
            } 
            
            // Case 2: Middle element is smaller than the last element
            // → Right half is sorted, so minimum lies in left half (including mid)
            else if (nums[mid] < nums[end]) {
                end = mid;
            } 
            
            // Case 3: nums[mid] == nums[end]
            // → Cannot determine the side due to duplicates
            // → Safely reduce search space by shrinking end
            else {
                end--;
            }
        }

        // At the end, start == end → pointing to minimum element
        return nums[start];
    }
}
🧠 Intuition (very important)
You compare with nums[end] to detect which half is sorted
If duplicates block the decision → shrink search space safely
⏱ Time Complexity
Case	Complexity
Best / Average	O(log n)
Worst (many duplicates)	O(n)

👉 Worst case example:

[1,1,1,1,1]

You only reduce end-- one step at a time.

💾 Space Complexity
O(1) (no extra memory used)
